  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level ridge now off the East Coast as a closed upper low drops \ninto the Great Lakes from the Canadian prairies and northern plains \nregions. A cold front will be moving eastward through the \nMississippi Valley, but a couple more days of dry and mild weather \nwill continue for our area.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAs mentioned above, the cold front is slowing up over the past \ncouple of synoptic model runs, and is now a Sunday night feature for \nthe southern Great Lakes. Line of showers to accompany the front, \nlikely anafrontal, and will be scattered in nature. Low level \nforcing turning frontolytic as it moves through our CWA, and as a \nresult, QPF is on the low side and should generally be less than a \ntenth of an inch. Sharply cooler in a Canadian airmass with broad \nhigh pressure building in behind this low pressure system as \ntemperatures swing from well above to below normal for the second \nhalf of November.",
